    "level": "beginner",
    "tier": "secondary",
    "locale": "en",
    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nA barter exchange assumes that each party wants what the other is offering. A more widely accepted medium of exchange makes it possible to separate the sale of one good from the purchase of another.\n\nExplanation\n- The problem is about needs having to meet at the same time.\n- An intermediary of exchange reduces this constraint.\n- Historical theories of the origin of money remain distinct from this teaching model.\n\nLimits and confusions\nThis model explains a difficulty of direct exchange; it does not prove that every society historically moved from barter to money.",
