    "level": "beginner",
    "tier": "secondary",
    "locale": "en",
    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nAfter 1944, Bretton Woods organized a system in which the dollar was convertible into gold for central banks and the other currencies were pegged to the dollar.\n\nExplanation\n- It gave the United States a central international monetary role.\n- The system depended on American discipline in maintaining convertibility and sufficient reserves.\n- Tensions from deficits and money issuance led to the Nixon Shock.\n\nLimits and confusions\nGold convertibility applied to foreign monetary authorities, not as an exchange guarantee for each individual. The parities were not immutable.",
