    "locale": "en",
    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nA banknote or certificate convertible into metal makes it possible to transfer a claim rather than carry gold or silver. Its holder then depends on the custodian's ability to honor the conversion.\n\nExplanation\n- Portability improves, but an intermediary keeps the metal.\n- Full backing must be distinguished from issuance exceeding the reserves.\n- A restriction or suspension of conversion changes the nature of the promise.\n\nLimits and confusions\nA promise of conversion depends on the contract and on the issuer's reserves. Do not generalize this mechanism to the origin of all paper currencies.",
