    "content": "Short answer\nAn interest rate expresses the cost of credit or the agreed return on a loan over a period of time. It depends notably on risk, inflation expectations, market conditions and monetary policy.\n\nExplanation\n- The same figure does not mean the same thing depending on the term and the conditions of the contract.\n- Austrian analysis studies how rates coordinate saving and investment.\n- Its critiques of artificially low rates are an interpretation of cycles, not proof that every rate cut causes a crisis.\n\nLimits and confusions\nDistinguish nominal rate from real rate, credit risk and term. The interpretation of rates as coordinating savings belongs notably to Austrian analysis.",
