    "locale": "en",
    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nLayered money describes a system in which a base layer (gold, reserves or central bank money) supports upper layers of banknotes, deposits and claims.\n\nExplanation\n- In some historical convertibility systems, monetary claims could rest on other claims, themselves convertible into metal.\n- Each layer adds usefulness and liquidity, but also counterparty risk.\n- Modern fiat keeps layers, but without a final metallic anchor.\n\nLimits and confusions\nMonetary layers built on claims are not the same as Bitcoin payment protocols. Each layer must be examined according to its settlement and custody mechanisms.",
