    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nThe Nixon Shock refers to the 1971 suspension of the dollar's convertibility into gold, which ended the Bretton Woods peg and accelerated the modern fiat era.\n\nExplanation\n- Dollars could no longer be exchanged for gold by other countries.\n- The event removed an important constraint on American monetary expansion.\n- It marks a major break in the monetary history of the 20th century.\n- It feeds Bitcoin analyses about non-manipulable money and limited supply.\n\nLimits and confusions\nThe 1971 suspension concerns the official international convertibility of the dollar into gold. The transformation of the monetary system extends beyond this single decision.",
