    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nQuantitative easing (QE) is a policy in which a central bank buys financial assets on a large scale in order to inject liquidity, lower certain interest rates and support the economy or the markets.\n\nExplanation\n- QE increases the central bank's balance sheet.\n- It can support asset prices and financial conditions.\n- Its distributional effects and incentives are controversial.\n- It feeds criticism about monetary inflation, Cantillon effects and market dependence.\n\nLimits and confusions\nAn increase in bank reserves does not mechanically translate into an equal increase in credit or consumer prices. The effects depend on the context.",
