    "content": "Short answer\nRedenomination consists of replacing a currency with a new unit by removing zeros or changing the nominal scale. It can simplify prices but does not solve the monetary causes if policy remains unchanged.\n\nExplanation\n- It often happens after high inflation.\n- It improves accounting readability but not necessarily purchasing power.\n- Without monetary discipline, the zeros can come back.\n- Latin American cases illustrate the repetition and limits of this strategy.\n\nLimits and confusions\nChanging the scale of prices and balances does not in itself increase their real purchasing power. A redenomination and an economic stabilization are 2 different operations.",
