    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nSound money refers to money that is hard to devalue, reliable over time and able to preserve economic calculation and savings.\n\nExplanation\n- Historically, it is associated with coins of reliable weight and purity, then with gold.\n- It stands in contrast to clipped, debased or over-issued currencies.\n- In Bitcoin discourse, it refers to money that is scarce, verifiable and resistant to manipulation.\n\nLimits and confusions\n\"Sound money\" is a normative concept whose criteria need to be made explicit. A supply that is hard to increase does not guarantee a stable price or constant purchasing power.",
    "parentIds": [
      "bitcoin-monetary-policy",
      "store-of-value"
    ],
    "relatedIds": [
      "bitcoin-monetary-policy",
      "store-of-value"
    ],
    "reviewMethod": "semantic_light",
    "sources": [
      "PlanB his205 — History of Coinage"
    ],
