    "locale": "en",
    "sourceLocale": "fr",
    "translationStatus": "machine",
    "content": "Short answer\nStablecoin risk criteria make it possible to evaluate a stablecoin beyond its yield or popularity: peg maintenance, reserve quality, transparency, audits, liquidity, redemption mechanisms, integrations, legal framework and censorship risk. The criteria vary depending on whether the stablecoin is fiat-backed, crypto-collateralized or algorithmic.\n\nExplanation\n- For a fiat-backed stablecoin, look at reserves, liquidity, account segregation, audits and redemption capacity.\n- For a crypto-backed stablecoin, look at overcollateralization, liquidations, oracle risk, governance and market depth.\n- Centralization can improve regulatory clarity but increases the risk of freezing or censorship.\n- A stablecoin has to be evaluated according to its use: trading, DeFi, treasury, payments, arbitrage or dollar exposure.\n\nLimits and confusions\nAn attestation at a given date, a reserve report and a full audit do not have the same scope. No single criterion guarantees the peg or access to redemption.",
