    "content": "Short answer\nStablecoin centralization risk comes from the issuer, the bank reserves, the smart contracts, the blacklist functions, the exchanges and the legal framework.\n\nExplanation\n- A centralized stablecoin can be frozen or censored.\n- The user depends on the issuer for redemption and peg maintenance.\n- Compliance can come into tension with censorship resistance.\n- This risk is the main difference from Bitcoin.\n\nLimits and confusions\nFreezing powers and redemption conditions vary with the token and its governance. Keeping the peg does not erase the censorship risk.",
